WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.680
And finally, to complete our shot,

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.599
we need to create a Cinema Camera.

3
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:08.560
This is the view in which the player will

4
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.150
see this whole sequence unfold.

5
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.310
So if we jump back to our sequence right now,

6
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.710
essentially all we have is just the character's movement.

7
00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:17.989
In fact, the camera that we're viewing through

8
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:20.550
is essentially just the perspective camera

9
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:21.629
within Unreal.

10
00:00:21.629 --> 00:00:24.269
So if we were to play the sequence back,

11
00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:26.309
say at runtime or in a game,

12
00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:28.969
there's really nothing that's going to control

13
00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:30.789
the player seeing this.

14
00:00:30.809 --> 00:00:34.500
So to do that, we need to add a Cinema Camera.

15
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.979
So we could go to Track,

16
00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:38.359
and we could add an Actor to Sequence

17
00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:40.899
if we had the Cinema Camera in our world,

18
00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:44.020
or we could also add a Camera Cut Track.

19
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.740
But in this case, all I'm going to do

20
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.060
is go up to our Place Actors,

21
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.100
so underneath Windows, Place Actors,

22
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.560
and I'm going to search for Cinema,

23
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.280
and I'm going to drag this Cinema Camera Actor

24
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.640
into our sequence.

25
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.119
Now, before I let go and drag this in there,

26
00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:00.600
I want you to notice that the only thing

27
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.679
we have in our sequence right now

28
00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:03.299
is the SKM Bot.

29
00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:06.400
So as soon as I drag this in there,

30
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.540
there we go,

31
00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.079
you'll see that it not only adds the Cinema Camera,

32
00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:10.920
so I'll roll these up,

33
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.799
so we have our Cinema Camera Actor,

34
00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:15.780
and now we have a Camera Cut Track.

35
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.959
So if you recall at the beginning of this series

36
00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:20.120
when we were having this class,

37
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.480
you'll notice that when I open up the sequence

38
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.840
and I click this little Play button,

39
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.840
you see that I actually viewed through the camera,

40
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.959
which is essentially what we're doing.

41
00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:29.920
So I'm going to close my Place Actors tab,

42
00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:32.700
and you'll notice that the camera itself

43
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.840
is off on the end here,

44
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.239
so I'm actually going to stop piloting this

45
00:01:36.239 --> 00:01:38.859
and move this around,

46
00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:40.459
and we'll go back to our shot here,

47
00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:43.579
and one other thing as a pro tip to keep in mind

48
00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:48.290
is by default, so if I'm in my viewport here,

49
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.769
and I click the little hamburger stack here,

50
00:01:49.769 --> 00:01:51.390
the three patties,

51
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.489
you'll notice that Allow Cinematic Control is on by default.

52
00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:56.390
If you turn this off,

53
00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.329
it's going to stop whenever you open up a sequence

54
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:00.890
or you open up a camera,

55
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.109
it's going to stop the editor

56
00:02:02.109 --> 00:02:05.750
from automatically selecting or controlling that viewport,

57
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:06.590
which is what we want,

58
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.509
because I want this just to be my perspective,

59
00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.639
I want my right side to be my actual shot.

60
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:15.020
All right, so now if I select my Camera Cuts Track

61
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.860
and you'll see my Cinema Camera Actor,

62
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.000
I can click this little plus,

63
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.240
and now we see the camera,

64
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.259
which in this case is at zero, zero, zero,

65
00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:23.439
which is under the world.

66
00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:24.780
Not what we want.

67
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.139
So here's another little tip.

68
00:02:26.139 --> 00:02:27.819
So if you notice a little camera icon

69
00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.180
that says Unlock Viewport Camera Cut.

70
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.180
So if I unclick this,

71
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.659
so I'm no longer viewing,

72
00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:35.099
but if I go down to my Cinema Camera Actor,

73
00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:39.099
and this time I click the little camera icon next to it,

74
00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:41.159
you'll see that I'm actually piloting

75
00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.400
the Cinema Camera Actor.

76
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.599
In fact, I can do the same thing.

77
00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:45.379
So if I eject out of here,

78
00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:46.479
I could go perspective,

79
00:02:46.479 --> 00:02:48.080
select my Cinema Camera Actor,

80
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.810
or go over into my world, right click,

81
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.389
and I should be able to pilot this,

82
00:02:54.389 --> 00:02:55.930
which is not there in the right click,

83
00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.050
but that's totally fine.

84
00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:59.069
We'll just do it through here because truth be told,

85
00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:00.430
when you're working with sequences,

86
00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.509
most of the time you are in this interface.

87
00:03:02.509 --> 00:03:04.289
So selecting back and forth between this

88
00:03:04.289 --> 00:03:06.870
is a lot faster than navigating your outliner

89
00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.469
or the viewport.

90
00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:09.310
All right.

91
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:10.849
So with our Cinema Camera Actor selected,

92
00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:14.389
and the camera itself locked to the viewport,

93
00:03:14.389 --> 00:03:17.129
now I'm piloting this so I can move our camera up

94
00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.949
just like we would with anything else.

95
00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:22.069
And let's find our little platform,

96
00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:23.669
which is over here.

97
00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:25.310
There's our little character.

98
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.849
So now let's get our camera

99
00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:30.469
roughly into position where we want it.

100
00:03:30.469 --> 00:03:31.310
Perfect.

101
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.409
So we'll do something like this, like an overhead shot.

102
00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:34.250
Now at this juncture,

103
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.409
not going into depth or details

104
00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:40.150
about establishing cameras or using different zoom lengths

105
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.069
to establish different moods.

106
00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:44.030
But in this case, we can just keep the default

107
00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.389
or why not?

108
00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:46.229
Feel free to go ahead.

109
00:03:46.229 --> 00:03:47.210
You can change your film back,

110
00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.169
maybe your lens settings and your zoom.

111
00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:51.169
That's absolutely fine.

112
00:03:51.169 --> 00:03:52.009
All right.

113
00:03:52.009 --> 00:03:53.370
So now with our Camera Actor there,

114
00:03:53.389 --> 00:03:56.150
I'm gonna go ahead and move my playback head here.

115
00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.729
Let's go back to the beginning.

116
00:03:57.729 --> 00:04:01.009
So here's our Camera Actor expanded out.

117
00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:03.330
I'll go ahead and save our sequence that we have right now

118
00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.689
and with my Camera Actor,

119
00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:07.009
I'm going to expand out the transform.

120
00:04:07.009 --> 00:04:09.530
So again, let's get this roughly into position

121
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.250
where we want it.

122
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.090
My transform, click our little plus key button right here.

123
00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.339
We'll move to where the character jumps off.

124
00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:18.180
So maybe what I wanna do

125
00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.139
is just have the camera come to a rest.

126
00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:22.819
So now I'm just gonna pilot it, move it forward.

127
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:25.160
And you'll notice as soon as I stop moving,

128
00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.160
because we have auto key turned on, I get my key frames.

129
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.000
All right.

130
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:29.819
So let's just see how that looks.

131
00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:32.879
Let's go back to the beginning and let's play.

132
00:04:32.879 --> 00:04:35.519
You follow the character and he jumps off.

133
00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:38.250
Great, fairly good movement.

134
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.470
Another little pro tip here

135
00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.550
that when you have your viewport set,

136
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.350
so if I actually go up to perspective here,

137
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.389
cinematic viewport, as opposed to default,

138
00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:49.709
I will have my playback control.

139
00:04:49.709 --> 00:04:51.790
So these are your exact same playback controls

140
00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.029
as my sequencer.

141
00:04:53.089 --> 00:04:55.589
And I can scrub through, I can play, I can go back,

142
00:04:55.589 --> 00:04:57.009
which is extremely handy.

143
00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.170
But I also have some other tools here for like a grid.

144
00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.129
So for example, if I turn on my grid shot here,

145
00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:04.949
just so I can see my three by three grid,

146
00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:08.209
and I follow my character for rules of composition,

147
00:05:08.209 --> 00:05:10.829
maybe I don't like how this end shot ends

148
00:05:10.829 --> 00:05:12.629
because he's kind of getting closer

149
00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:13.970
to the left side of the frame.

150
00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.730
Again, purely just an aesthetic choice.

151
00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.610
But I could easily change that by doing this little trick.

152
00:05:20.629 --> 00:05:23.430
So I'm going to select my transform keys.

153
00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.149
I'm gonna go to my animation curve editor.

154
00:05:26.149 --> 00:05:27.670
And what I'm gonna do here,

155
00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.730
let me kind of move this off screen.

156
00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.670
We'll reselect it here in a second,

157
00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:33.649
just so we can see our character.

158
00:05:33.649 --> 00:05:34.509
There we go.

159
00:05:34.509 --> 00:05:37.470
So select my transform, you'll see it activates the keys.

160
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.910
And what I'm looking for, if we look at our axes here,

161
00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.810
is Z is gonna be our up direction.

162
00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:46.069
So maybe I wanna take my rotation of my,

163
00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:48.779
which should be my yaw here.

164
00:05:48.779 --> 00:05:50.899
And what I wanna do is actually just rotate this slightly.

165
00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.199
So what I'm doing is grabbing this key,

166
00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:54.160
which I'm gonna do this real quick,

167
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.519
and I'm holding down shift and middle mouse dragging.

168
00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:01.300
And what that allows me to do is move the keyframe

169
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:05.360
and keep it snapped on the actual frame itself, so 135.

170
00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.120
The other thing you could do is select it and drag,

171
00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.680
but you'll see that it starts to move any way free form.

172
00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:11.819
So again, either way that you wanna do it,

173
00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:14.399
I'm gonna left click, drag, select the key,

174
00:06:14.399 --> 00:06:17.639
and I'm gonna shift and middle click, drag,

175
00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:19.779
just to keep him in that third.

176
00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:21.040
Go ahead and save this.

177
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.379
And now we'll go back to our playback.

178
00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:28.029
We'll see that the player starts to run and then he jumps.

179
00:06:28.029 --> 00:06:28.990
So just a little bit better movement.

180
00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.949
Again, purely an aesthetic choice.

181
00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:33.029
You can do whatever you would like with it.

182
00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:35.149
But now we have our camera cut.

183
00:06:35.149 --> 00:06:38.180
We have our cinema camera actor, which is great.

184
00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.060
We have our movement of our character.

185
00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:40.939
We can save this.

186
00:06:40.939 --> 00:06:41.779
In fact, to validate,

187
00:06:41.779 --> 00:06:44.379
we can close the sequence to make sure everything works.

188
00:06:44.379 --> 00:06:47.699
I can go back to my sequence tab, open my 105 EX,

189
00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:49.019
and you'll see here we have it.

190
00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.680
Now, again, if we start playing back,

191
00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.579
you'll notice we just have a camera,

192
00:06:52.579 --> 00:06:55.279
which is not what we want in the composition.

193
00:06:55.279 --> 00:06:58.759
So go back to our sequence on our camera cuts track,

194
00:06:58.759 --> 00:07:02.079
click this, which is going to lock this to our viewport.

195
00:07:02.079 --> 00:07:05.839
And now when we play, we'll see the character runs.

196
00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:08.519
He jumps and he falls.

197
00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:09.600
Great, perfect.

198
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.519
Now, one other tweak that I wanna make is

199
00:07:11.519 --> 00:07:14.160
it might not be readily visible through the video,

200
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.720
but you'll notice that the character is slightly blurry.

201
00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.720
And that's because our focus range of our camera

202
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.699
is actually set a little far.

203
00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:24.889
So we can actually control that down here very easily.

204
00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:27.009
So you can see that there are a few settings

205
00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:29.829
with our camera, and this is a great point

206
00:07:29.829 --> 00:07:32.750
to kind of explain that in Sequencer,

207
00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.449
we have a lot of control that we can take.

208
00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:38.029
In fact, there are a lot of properties and parameters

209
00:07:38.029 --> 00:07:40.629
that can be keyed, can be controlled, et cetera,

210
00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:43.290
but you may only see a few of them

211
00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.350
inside of the sequence when it's active.

212
00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.110
So what I'm gonna do is actually select

213
00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:48.910
my cinema camera actor, which essentially

214
00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.329
just selects it in my outliner, go to my details panel.

215
00:07:52.329 --> 00:07:54.350
And one of the first things to look at is

216
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.230
anytime you see this little kind of triangle

217
00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.110
or rotated square, however you wanna do it

218
00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:01.060
with a plus button, that just means

219
00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.160
that simply add a key frame for that property.

220
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.740
So in this case, what I wanna actually change

221
00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.060
is my focus distance.

222
00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.420
We're gonna focus settings, you'll see here,

223
00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:13.300
manual focus distance is what is set

224
00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.000
and is currently at a thousand centimeters.

225
00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.199
So if we turn on our draw debug focus plane,

226
00:08:18.199 --> 00:08:20.019
you'll see that it's way off in the distance.

227
00:08:20.019 --> 00:08:23.259
I can use my little eyedropper, so just left click once,

228
00:08:23.259 --> 00:08:26.240
go in and then pro tip here, middle mouse click

229
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.540
will actually select it or you can left click,

230
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.519
which should move it.

231
00:08:30.519 --> 00:08:33.320
All right, so about roughly 977 centimeters

232
00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.279
away from the camera is my character.

233
00:08:35.279 --> 00:08:38.279
So now if I turn off my draw debug focus plane,

234
00:08:38.279 --> 00:08:40.720
you see he's much more in focus,

235
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.100
a lot more crisp, which is what we want.

236
00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.779
Now, if we found, because I've set this

237
00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:49.000
on the actual camera itself, but I haven't key framed it,

238
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.039
it just sets it as its value.

239
00:08:51.039 --> 00:08:54.059
So if I scrub through and let's say at this juncture,

240
00:08:54.059 --> 00:08:55.539
he does get a little blurry.

241
00:08:55.539 --> 00:08:57.899
In fact, let's do our draw debug focus plane.

242
00:08:57.899 --> 00:09:01.059
Maybe he's a little outside of that, it doesn't look good.

243
00:09:01.059 --> 00:09:03.659
We could add a key frame, so if I do this real quick,

244
00:09:03.659 --> 00:09:05.460
you'll see that it creates a key

245
00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.080
on this manual focus distance and we could modify that.

246
00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:12.120
So I could grab this key, move it back real quick

247
00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.799
to the beginning, we have to hold down shift and snap,

248
00:09:15.799 --> 00:09:16.840
drag to the end here.

249
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.360
And maybe I just want to move this out slightly.

250
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.200
There we go, I have my second key.

251
00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.879
And now you'll see that that focus stays relatively

252
00:09:24.879 --> 00:09:28.389
within the position of where our character's at.

253
00:09:28.389 --> 00:09:29.669
The other thing to keep in mind here too,

254
00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:31.909
is as we established our camera,

255
00:09:31.909 --> 00:09:33.350
we don't necessarily want this camera

256
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.750
to exist in the world, at least for this example.

257
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.669
Now you could if you wanted to,

258
00:09:37.669 --> 00:09:40.470
but we want it to be a spawnable actor.

259
00:09:40.509 --> 00:09:42.590
And if you notice that we didn't actually have

260
00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.389
to right click on this and create a spawnable,

